:name: E1M-AEN801 (Alif Ensemble E8) M55-HP smoke
:description: Boot a single Cortex-M55-HP Zephyr slice headless, no hardware.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Variables filled in by `tan renode` (tan-cli, crates/tan-core/src/
# renode.rs -> build_renode_argv).  The wrapper injects `$elf` (the
# resolved <build_dir>/zephyr/zephyr.elf), `$repl` (the platform
# descriptor) and (per tan-cli#65) `$vtor` via `renode -e "$elf=@..."`
# BEFORE this script runs.  The `?=` defaults below only apply when a
# variable is still unset, so any wrapper-supplied value wins -- and the
# script stays runnable by hand from an alp-build'd hello-world tree:
#
#     renode metadata/renode/alif_ensemble_e8.resc
#
# This script now derives its own `$vtor` default from the loaded ELF's
# symbol table (see the comment at the `$vtor ?=` line below), so a bare
# hand run no longer needs one passed in either.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
$repl ?= @metadata/renode/alif_ensemble_e8.repl
$elf  ?= @examples/peripheral-io/hello-world/build/m55_hp-zephyr/zephyr/zephyr.elf

mach create "aen801"
machine LoadPlatformDescription $repl

# Surface the UART5 console.  Run headless (the wrapper passes
# `--console --disable-xwt`), so Renode routes the analyzer to stdout
# instead of a GUI window; the wrapper tees that stdout to --log, which
# is where a log-grep for the app's "[hello] done" marker would look.
# NB: no gate greps it today -- every renode workflow's boot-and-assert
# step is a no-op `echo` (#955), so nothing here is currently asserted.
showAnalyzer uart5

# Load the Zephyr ELF, then seed the vector-table base BEFORE `start`
# so Renode takes SP/PC from [$vtor] / [$vtor+4] instead of guessing.
# Order matters: `VectorTableOffset` set after `start` is too late, the
# initial values are latched when emulation begins; the lookup below
# also needs the ELF already loaded (it reads the ELF's own symbol
# table via GetSymbolAddress), so it comes after LoadELF too.
sysbus LoadELF $elf

# Vector-table base.  Left alone, Renode does NOT read this from the
# ELF: it guesses the LOWEST `vaddr` across the LOAD segments.  That is
# wrong for any image whose vector table is not in its lowest-addressed
# segment -- an MRAM-linked Zephyr app is exactly that shape, because
# its `.data` init image has a RAM `vaddr` (0x20000000, where .data is
# copied TO) paired with an MRAM `paddr` (where the initialiser
# actually lives).  Renode guesses that 0x20000000, reads SP/PC out of
# memory nothing was ever loaded into, gets zeros, and halts the CPU
# before executing an instruction (#947).
#
# Ask Renode for the real address instead of computing one.
# `_vector_table` is Zephyr's vector-table symbol
# (arch/arm/core/cortex_m/vector_table.S) and survives in every Zephyr
# .elf -- the default build never strips it.  `sysbus GetSymbolAddress`
# reads it straight out of the just-loaded ELF's own symbol table, so
# the value is right for whatever $elf actually is: the ITCM-linked
# shape, the MRAM-linked shape, an MCUboot-chained slot, or a future
# partition layout -- with no arithmetic here to fall out of sync with
# the board's Kconfig.
#
# What used to be here derived $vtor from the devicetree
# (`zephyr,code-partition = &slot0_partition`, partition@10000 of MRAM
# -> 0x80010000) as if that property placed the image.  It does not for
# the default $elf: `zephyr,code-partition` only takes effect through
# CONFIG_USE_DT_CODE_PARTITION, which has no `default y`
# (zephyr/Kconfig.zephyr) and is `select`ed only by BOOTLOADER_MCUBOOT
# (modules/Kconfig.mcuboot: `select USE_DT_CODE_PARTITION if
# !MCUBOOT_BOOTLOADER_MODE_RAM_LOAD`) and BOOTLOADER_BOSSA -- neither of
# which hello-world's empty prj.conf or this board's Kconfig.defconfig
# turns on.  So CONFIG_FLASH_LOAD_OFFSET keeps its plain `default 0`
# (zephyr/Kconfig.zephyr) and the image links at the MRAM base,
# 0x80000000, not the partition offset.  Verified two ways against the
# current default $elf, on the pinned Renode v1.16.1 (d66b0c2a):
#
#     arm-zephyr-eabi-nm zephyr.elf | grep vector_table
#         -> 80000000 T _vector_table
#     sysbus GetSymbolAddress "_vector_table"   (after `sysbus LoadELF`)
#         -> 0x0000000080000000
#
# A `0x80010000` VTOR (the old default) lands 64 KiB inside this
# image's own `text` section (0x800007c0..0x80012d78) -- so it would
# have read SP/PC out of code words instead of out of unloaded memory,
# but just as wrong.
#
# IMPORTANT -- what this still does and does not buy you on the pinned
# Renode v1.16.1.  Seeding VTOR from the real symbol fixes the
# HALT-on-zero: PC/SP now come from the image's actual vector table
# (confirmed above) instead of from memory nothing was loaded into.  It
# does NOT make a high-VTOR (MRAM-linked) image complete a boot,
# because of the separate v1.16.1 Cortex-M55 defect the .repl documents
# (it mis-executes a vector table placed at a high address like the
# MRAM base).  Run directly against this default $elf on the pinned
# build: PC/SP seed to the real entry point and the CPU starts
# executing, then MemManageFault-storms rather than reaching the app.
# To actually observe a completed boot under this Renode pin, build the
# ITCM-linked shape instead (tests/renode/aen_m55_itcm_run.overlay,
# `zephyr,flash = &itcm`) -- its `_vector_table` resolves to 0x0 and
# that shape runs cleanly (confirmed: PC/SP progress normally, no
# fault).  The lookup below handles both automatically; no `-e
# $vtor=...` override is needed for either shape any more.
#
# Assign it UNQUOTED -- a quoted `$vtor="0x80010000"` used to fail with
# `Cannot convert type 'string' to 'uint'`; GetSymbolAddress already
# returns a bare numeric value, so this stays true for the lookup too.
$vtor ?= `sysbus GetSymbolAddress "_vector_table"`
cpu VectorTableOffset $vtor

start
